<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=kuk-swamp">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kuk Swamp</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 7000 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kuk Swamp is an archaeological site in the Wahgi Valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a> highlands, at about 1,550 metres, some 12–13 kilometres northeast of Mount Hagen. Drainage ditches dug there from about 9,000 years ago, from major disposal channels to small field ditches, turned the swamp into grassland where taro was grown, and bananas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane were cultivated from 6,900–6,400 years ago. Studied by the Australian archaeologist Jack Golson, it was made a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2008 as one of the places where people developed agriculture independently. The Kawelka people live in the area today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kuk-swamp.jpg" alt="Kuk Swamp" width="330" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA: JPL · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kuk_New_Guinea_2002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
